# CHANGELOG.md is APPEND-ONLY: every PR adds its own `### ...` entry under the shared
# `## Unreleased` anchor, so a conflict here is two PRs both appending, never two PRs
# disagreeing about the same line. Union keeps both sides instead of stopping the merge.
#
# GITHUB DOES NOT HONOUR THIS. It affects LOCAL merges only. Measured 2026-08-08 on this
# repo, after the attribute was on main: 0 of 19 CONFLICTING PRs flipped to MERGEABLE,
# across TWO settled trigger-and-read cycles (GitHub computes mergeability lazily, so one
# unchanged reading is indistinguishable from a stale one and was not trusted alone). The
# real server-side merge agrees with the flag: `gh pr update-branch` refused cp#332 AND
# cp#350, 2 of 2, both of which merge CLEAN locally under this attribute. Positive control,
# same token and session: the same command SUCCEEDED on cp#353 and advanced its branch, so
# the refusals are the server's answer and not a broken tool.
#
# THIS FILE DOES NOT DRAIN THE LEGACY QUEUE. An earlier version of this comment claimed it
# did, and that claim was wrong. Measured 2026-08-08 on cp#336: git reads a file's merge
# attribute from the PRE-MERGE working tree. Draining means checking out the PR branch and
# merging main into it, so the tree git consults is the PR BRANCH, and every legacy PR was
# authored before this file existed (`git cat-file -e cae0bd9e:.gitattributes` -> missing).
# The attribute is therefore absent at the moment it would have to apply, `git check-attr
# merge -- CHANGELOG.md` reports `unspecified`, and the merge conflicts exactly as if this
# file were not here.
#
# THE ONE RULE, which every observation below is just a consequence of:
#
#     Every git merge operation resolves a file's merge attribute from the CHECKED-OUT
#     WORKING TREE, never from the commits being merged. `merge` and `merge-tree` alike.
#
# Consequences, each of which fooled a first pass:
#   - `git check-attr` run AFTER a conflicted merge reports `union`, because the merge
#     itself pulled this file in from main. It is describing a state that did not exist
#     when the driver was chosen. Query it on the clean pre-merge checkout or not at all.
#   - Merging the PR head INTO a main checkout DOES apply union and looks like proof. It is
#     the opposite direction from a drain: there the tree git consults is main, which
#     carries the attribute. Direction is not a property of the merge; it is which side you
#     checked out.
#   - A CLONE SITTING ON A STALE `main` BEHAVES LIKE A CLONE WITH NO ATTRIBUTE AT ALL. This
#     one produced a false claim that got as far as a merged comment: `git merge-tree` was
#     reported here as unable to honour the driver. It honours it. Supplied via
#     `-c core.attributesFile`, merge-tree on cp#336's two parents returns rc=0 and a tree
#     byte-identical to the real merge commit's (`01e7da0d`). The earlier test had run from
#     a working tree parked on a main that predated this file, and `check-attr` printed
#     `unspecified` in the same output that was read as "the tool ignores it".
#     Caught by strummer during the cp#336 re-review, by checking rather than accepting.
#
# The failure shape worth remembering: an anomaly was explained by inventing a CAPABILITY
# LIMIT in the tool when the real cause was STATE the operator controlled. Prefer the
# explanation that implicates your own checkout until it is ruled out.
#
# THE RECIPE THAT ACTUALLY WORKS supplies the attribute from OUTSIDE the branch, so it does
# not care what the PR branch predates. Preferred form, used to drain cp#336 live:
#
#     printf 'CHANGELOG.md merge=union\n' > /tmp/drain-attrs
#     git -c core.attributesFile=/tmp/drain-attrs merge origin/main
#
# This is per-invocation and mutates NO shared state. Prefer it: a clone here is a singleton
# shared across parallel contexts, so appending to its `.git/info/attributes` changes merge
# behaviour for every other session using that clone, and leaves residue if you forget to
# restore it.
#
# The repo-local form also works and is fine in a clone you own alone:
#
#     echo 'CHANGELOG.md merge=union' >> "$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/info/attributes"
#
# Use --git-common-dir, NOT --git-dir: inside a linked worktree --git-dir resolves to
# .git/worktrees/<name>, whose info/attributes git does not consult, and the attribute
# silently fails to arm (that failure is what made the first negative control meaningless --
# both arms conflicted because the positive arm had never armed).
#
# Verified bidirectionally on cp#336, same commits both arms, attribute the only variable:
# with it `rc=0` and zero conflict markers; with it removed `rc=1` and one marker.
#
# UNION IS A DUMB DRIVER -- rc=0 IS NOT THE CHECK. It keeps both sides unconditionally and
# cannot tell a correct append from a structural mistake, so a conflict-free result can still
# be wrong. After any union merge of this file, verify: exactly one `## Unreleased` anchor, no
# conflict markers, no entry the MERGE duplicated (pre-existing duplicates are not yours), and
# nothing dropped from either side. On cp#336 the honest check was arithmetic: 2872 + 8 = 2880
# lines, and `git diff HEAD^1 HEAD -- CHANGELOG.md` showed exactly main's one new entry.
#
# DRAIN LOCALLY RATHER THAN `gh pr update-branch`, EVEN WHERE UPDATE-BRANCH WOULD SUCCEED.
# Measured on cp#336: pushing a real merge commit left both prior approvals STALE, which is
# honest and forces a fresh review. `gh pr update-branch` instead CARRIES the existing review's
# commit_id forward onto the new merge commit, so the PR reports approved-at-head for a merge
# no reviewer ever saw (that is how vivijure-local#305 came to look landable). A commit_id
# comparison cannot detect it: the equality it tests is exactly what the carry-forward produces.
# Compare the review's submitted_at against the head commit's creation time instead.
#
# SO WHY IS THIS FILE STILL HERE. One narrow live effect: cp#358's changelog-entry check
# accepts EITHER a CHANGELOG.md touch OR a changelog.d/ fragment during the migration
# window, so a branch cut from main AFTER 2026-08-07 may still edit CHANGELOG.md directly.
# Those branches DO carry this attribute, so union applies to them. That population is the
# only thing it serves.
#
# NOT THE REAL FIX. cp#358's changelog.d/ fragments are. They cover every NEW PR; this
# covers neither the backlog nor anything past the migration window.
#
# REMOVE THIS once the migration window closes and the legacy queue is drained. After that
# nothing should be touching CHANGELOG.md directly, so the attribute is pure hazard: it
# would silently change local merge behaviour for no remaining reason.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
